          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              121,
              125
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              110,
              111
            ],
            "before": "SHA-256 digest over those four values. `emit_tail_receipt` reads the sink's\r\ncached entries and head; it does not reread the file or recompute the entries.\r\nA caller comparing persisted snapshots must first reopen the store or run\r\n`verify_integrity` successfully, then emit the observed receipt. This sequence\r\nassumes one writer and controlled custody during the check. The sink can check consistency of its\r\n",
            "after": "SHA-256 digest over those four values. The sink can check consistency of its\r\n"
          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              137,
              139
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              122,
              123
            ],
            "before": "before calling it. Rejection is not a retention license. The sink's fixed\r\n`authority_effect` marker is the literal string `\"None\"`; it is neither JSON\r\n`null` nor producer authentication. Sensitive fields\r\n",
            "after": "before calling it. Rejection is not a retention license. Sensitive fields\r\n"
          },
          {
            "operation": "delete",
            "base_lines": [
              160,
              174
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              143,
              143
            ],
            "before": "There is also a failed-write boundary before a restart: `record_refusal`\r\nadvances the in-memory entry list and head before `_write_file` finishes.\r\nIf replacement raises an exception, the caller receives no successful append,\r\nbut the old sink object can emit a receipt for an entry absent from the\r\npersisted file. The new package regression injects that one failure and\r\nobserves this divergence. Discard or successfully reload that object and\r\nreconcile the attempted operation before retrying; an exception is not a\r\nrollback guarantee. This is a fault-injection example, not a crash campaign.\r\n\r\nIf each record has a bounded size B and N records are added individually,\r\nthe accumulated entry bytes written are of order B times N(N+1)/2, plus\r\nheaders, while a single final snapshot occupies order BN bytes. This simple\r\ncount assumes no batching or compaction and is not a measured benchmark.\r\nBound memory, record size, total retained bytes, and write failures separately.\r\n\r\n",
            "after": ""
          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              180,
              191
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              148,
              149
            ],
            "before": "value domain. For example, Python's code-point key order differs from JCS's\r\nUTF-16 code-unit order for some supplementary characters, and the baseline\r\nencoder leaves Python's permissive non-finite-number option enabled. JCS\r\nrequires rejection of NaN and infinity. These are concrete differences, not\r\nan assumption that sorted keys provide standards conformance. [RFC 8785][jcs]\r\n\r\nNormalization is also lossy: `Fraction(1, 3)` and the string `\"1/3\"` become\r\nthe same stored value; integer key `1` and string key `\"1\"` collide after key\r\nstringification. The sink therefore retains normalized proposal information,\r\nnot an injective encoding of every caller value. Preserve separately authorized\r\nraw bytes and their type/encoding contract when exact original reconstruction\r\nis required.\r\n",
            "after": "value domain. [RFC 8785][jcs]\r\n"
          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              205,
              207
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              162,
              164
            ],
            "before": "to the header. The exercised empty-file, chain, count, and head checks produce typed\r\nintegrity or truncation errors. This is not a claim that every malformed\r\nPython or JSON value follows that error taxonomy.\r\n",
            "after": "to the header. Empty or inconsistent files produce typed integrity or\r\ntruncation errors.\r\n"
          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              229,
              232
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              185,
              186
            ],
            "before": "and unknown header fields are not committed by this comparison. The baseline\r\nloader also does not validate the header's `schema` value. Replacing that\r\nvalue can leave the count/head comparison unchanged; an emitted receipt's\r\nschema does not retrospectively validate the file header. A caller\r\n",
            "after": "and unknown header fields are not committed by this comparison. A caller\r\n"
          },
          {
            "operation": "delete",
            "base_lines": [
              240,
              253
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              193,
              193
            ],
            "before": "\r\n| Persisted modification | Baseline reload | Comparison with retained count/head |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| Change a protected normalized payload, keep old digest | Rejects | No verified new snapshot |\r\n| Remove suffix, keep old header | Rejects | No verified new snapshot |\r\n| Remove suffix and write a matching header | Can pass | Different from the retained longer snapshot |\r\n| Add a valid next entry | Passes | Different; inspect the retained prefix to establish extension |\r\n| Change unbound header metadata or JSON whitespace | Can pass | Can match; raw-file digest can differ |\r\n\r\nThe hash assumption concerns replacement of protected encoded payloads\r\nagainst a retained digest. It does not turn normalizations that already map\r\ndifferent inputs to the same payload into detectable changes, and it does\r\nnot authenticate the anchor's custodian. A self-digest of a receipt can be\r\nrecomputed by anyone who can replace that receipt.\r\n",
            "after": ""
          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              266,
              268
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              205,
              206
            ],
            "before": "from pfp.infrastructure.persistence import cemetery_sink\r\n\r\nCemeterySink = cemetery_sink.CemeterySink\r\n",
            "after": "from pfp.infrastructure.persistence.cemetery_sink import CemeterySink\r\n"
          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              320,
              322
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              257,
              258
            ],
            "before": "a digest is a conditional identity test for the particular covered bytes and\r\nencoding, under the stated hash assumption. A hash of a label or normalized\r\nentry does not identify the entire original proposal or its semantic defect. Grouping by a normalized feature or\r\n",
            "after": "a digest establishes byte identity. Grouping by a normalized feature or\r\n"
          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              345,
              350
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              280,
              281
            ],
            "before": "present. A clean result means only no match on the supplied panel. The method returns\r\na decision and record; it does not mutate the frozen parent or clone, remove\r\nthem from a repository, or demonstrate that a host applied the proposed purge.\r\nThat consumer action is a separate integration obligation. Pattern execution\r\nalso needs appropriate input-size and resource limits; a syntactically valid\r\nregular expression is not a bounded runtime guarantee.\r\n",
            "after": "present. A clean result means only no match on the supplied panel.\r\n"
          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              415,
              416
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              345,
              347
            ],
            "before": "1/5, toxicity at or above the threshold synthesizes a record proposing a downgrade to\r\n`DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY`. Synthesis alone does not update the rule or host policy. Five records containing one pattern-only case give exactly\r\n",
            "after": "1/5, toxicity at or above the threshold synthesizes a downgrade to\r\n`DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY`. Five records containing one pattern-only case give exactly\r\n"
          },
          {
            "operation": "replace",
            "base_lines": [
              421,
              428
            ],
            "target_zero_based_slice": [
              351,
              352
            ],
            "before": "The trigger labels must themselves be justified. They describe signal presence,\r\nnot an independent oracle judgment that a proposal is valid or suppressed.\r\nRecords with neither signal still enter the explicit-interface denominator.\r\nFor one pattern-only record, two dual-signal records, and three no-signal\r\nrecords, this interface reports 1/6; the fraction conditional on a pattern\r\nmatch would instead be 1/3. Neither denominator is automatically the valid,\r\neligible-proposal denominator of PFP III's false-suppression experiment.\r\nAn empty trigger list produces zero by convention, not evidence of safety. A different input interface\r\n",
            "after": "The trigger labels must themselves be justified. A different input interface\r\n"
